> tl;dr
>
> - Small vendor shortcuts accrue quiet costs: a GP2022 field used spaces in machine names, breaking expected Drupal behavior.
> - Impact was ~10 hours for workarounds/corrections—not catastrophic, but avoidable and recurring.
> - In‑house leadership with vendors as partners has consistently produced better long‑term outcomes.

This is a small story with a big lesson. A single vendor choice—allowing spaces in machine names—broke a vanilla Drupal feature. The fix was straightforward, but the pattern is familiar: quiet costs that add up when quality isn’t owned.

We don’t avoid vendors; we set the standards and lead. That shift—from outsourcing ownership to partnering under our governance—has been the difference between fragile systems and ones that get stronger over time.

## Problem

A third‑party implementation used spaces in database keys/machine names. This violated basic standards and broke filtering for 2025 GP statements.

## Approach

- Built workarounds and corrections to recover the feature for the short term (~10 hours).
- Logged the debt and fed improvements into our standardization backlog.
- Advocated governance: vendors operate as partners under our coding and data standards.

## Key decisions

- Vendors are valuable partners but should not lead platform architecture for long‑lived systems.
- Maintain a concise, enforced set of coding/data standards for shared features.
- Favor shared components and structured models to keep quality high.

## Outcomes

- Feature unblocked with minimal user disruption.
- Reinforced the rationale for in‑house leadership and standardization of component libraries and content models.
- Reduced likelihood of repeat issues by tightening reviews and guidelines.

